  "body": "<p>Lirael’s note asks the company to find a woman who keeps fighting beneath withered leaves. <a href=\"#minerva\">Minerva</a> has traveled this way before, but familiarity does not settle the riddle. With <a href=\"#thorn\">Thorn</a> and <a href=\"#trougar\">Trougar</a>, she follows the Eternal River toward the Dark Shroud, carrying old disagreements into a landscape whose rules they still do not understand.</p><h2 id=\"song-river-and-child\">A warning on the river road</h2><p>Minerva asks the others to leave the Children of Viota alone unless attacked. Before they reach the mountain, one attacks anyway. It invokes Nythara and claims that someone knows what Minerva hides. Threads seal its mouth; cutting them reveals a missing tongue. The assault continues, with teleportation and a needle aimed at Minerva’s own tongue.</p><p>Minerva turns her skin poisonous, Thorn uses flame, and the attacker falls. Unlike the disappearances Minerva expects, its body remains. Trougar tries healing it, without success. Minerva contacts Vaulstren, who says he will consult papers. No answer identifying the sender or explaining this particular body arrives in the scene.</p><p>They keep moving. Trougar will not let Minerva put the body on his spider after he has refused. She carries it herself. The disagreement is unresolved as the party goes around the mountain and reaches the boundary of the Dark Shroud.</p><h2 id=\"song-wrong-tree\">The tree that does not answer</h2><p>A half-withered tree diverts their attention. Minerva addresses it through her ring; she feels a response from its living half, but it does not answer. Thorn tries her own approach. The narrator distinguishes this tree from the object of their search. Trougar’s impatience grows: he questions whether Minerva has brought them here for her own purposes. That is his suspicion, not a revealed motive.</p><p>Using her lens, Minerva sees an immobile man where the others see a tree. He does not respond. Old roots near him become a more useful clue. Trougar follows their direction deeper into the forest and finally sees a blade cutting through root creatures between the trees. He calls the others forward to help. Minerva follows, still wondering whether lowering the woman’s sword is the answer to the note.</p><h2 id=\"song-defender-and-stone\">Yen cannot leave the stone</h2><p>The clearing is torn with exposed roots and disturbed earth. A weathered stone stands in its center. Before it, Yen fights in battered armor, cutting through repeated waves. Children of Viota appear and seem to grow as they approach. She asks the strangers to stop at a distance until she can judge them.</p><p>Yen says the creatures must not touch the stone: they would become stronger and attack the village that banished her. Asked why she was expelled, she says she asked questions people would not answer. Why had the fey stopped bringing children to new life? Why did old roots remember spring without bearing seed? Why did people wait for mages to bring newcomers? Her account makes the defence a continuing obligation, without independently settling the history behind it.</p><p>Thorn becomes an earth elemental and helps hold the attackers. That gives Minerva room to read. The older Sylvan inscription joins falling, withering and new seed to a First Passage beyond the garden’s gates. It describes blossoms continuing while the making of seeds is lost, and warns against severing an older binding. A later inscription questions the right to pay such a price for life.</p><p>The words do not stop the attacks. Yen wants hope that understanding what happened might eventually let her lower her blade. She points the company toward a carpet seller in Larkhollow who remembers, or the four towers. Neither lead becomes a completed investigation here.</p><h2 id=\"song-mirror-and-departure\">The mirror shows a crossing</h2><p>Minerva promises to investigate. Yen cuts an opening through the next attackers so the company can leave; the guardian stays at the stone. There is no witnessed victory freeing her from the watch.</p><p>Before departing, Minerva gathers Thorn and Trougar around her mirror and asks why Viota, Keeper of the First Passage, crossed the river. The mirror answers with an image: a shadowed feminine figure crossing dark water beneath a starless sky. Flowers remain on the banks, but their seeds darken and break apart as the figure reaches the other side. The displayed words connect the crossing with seeds no longer taking hold.</p><p>The narrator narrows the result. They have seen the moment, not why it happened. Viota’s reason, the cause of the change and the truth behind Yen’s unanswered questions remain work for another journey. Minerva shares the book Fenwick gave her as the company considers what to do next.</p><p><strong>Continue reading</strong> <a href=\"#verdelune\">Verdelune</a> · <a href=\"#discovery-047\">Yen</a> · <a href=\"#viotas-monument\">Viota’s Monument</a> · <a href=\"#larkhollow\">Larkhollow</a></p>",
